sis, open inner parenthesis, a, plus c, close inner parenthesis, squared, close outer parenthesis, squared, end root, equals, open parenthesis, a, plus c, close parenthesis, squared, which equals a, squared, plus 2 a, c, plus c squared\"></span>.<!--[if--><!--[if--><!--[if--><!--[endif]--><!--[endif]--><!--[if--><!--[endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![if--><!--[if--><!--[endif]--><!--[endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![if--><!--![endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![if--><!--![if--><p>\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![if-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![if-->\n        <!--![if-->\n        <!--![if-->Choice A is incorrect and may be the result of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e117d3b8/img_008_5138adcc.png\" alt=\"the square root of, open parenthesis, a, plus c, close parenthesis, cubed, end root, divided by the square root of,  a, plus c, end root\"></span>. Choice B is incorrect and may be the result of incorrectly rewriting (<span class=\"italic\">a </span>+ <span class=\"italic\">c</span>)<sup>2</sup> as <span class=\"italic\">a</span><sup>2</sup> + <span class=\"italic\">c</span><sup>2</sup>. Choice D is incorrect and may be the result of incorrectly applying properties of exponents.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":9,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959635,"pPcc":"SAT#P","questionId":"e117d3b8","skill_cd":"P.A.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"Equivalent expressions","createDate":1691007959635,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Advanced Math","ibn":"08224-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"P","uId":"d246eaab-7366-4aa3-b329-734096f5c394","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"08224-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">If <span class=\"italic\">a</span> and <span class=\"italic\">c</span> are positive numbers, which of the following is equivalent to <span class=\"math_expression \"><span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of, open parenthesis, a plus c, close parenthesis, cubed, end root, times, the square root of a plus c, end root\"></span></span>?</p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<span class=\"math_expression \">\n            <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a plus c\"></span></span>\n"},"b":{"body":"<span class=\"math_expression \">\n            <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a squared plus c squared\"></span></span>\n"},"c":{"body":"<span class=\"math_expression \">\n            <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a squared plus 2 a c plus c squared\"></span></span>\n"},"d":{"body":"<span class=\"math_expression \">\n            <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"a squared times c squared\"></span></span>\n"}},"correct_choice":"c","rationale":"<p>Choice C is correct. Using the property that&nbsp;<span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of x, end root, times the square root of y, end root, equals the square root of x y, end root\"></span> for positive numbers <span class=\"italic\">x</span> and <span class=\"italic\">y</span>, with <span class=\"italic\">x</span> = (<span class=\"italic\">a </span>+<span class=\"italic\"> c</span>)<sup>3</sup> and <span class=\"italic\">y </span>= <span class=\"italic\">a</span> + <span class=\"italic\">c</span>, it follows that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of, open parenthesis, a, plus c, close parenthesis, cubed, end root, times, the square root of a, plus c, end root, equals the square root of, open parenthesis a, plus c, end parenthesis to the power 4, end root\"></span>. By rewriting (<span class=\"italic\">a</span> + <span class=\"italic\">c</span>)<sup>4</sup> as ((<span class=\"italic\">a</span> + <span class=\"italic\">c</span>)<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup>, it is possible to simplify the square root expression as follows: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of, open outer parenthesis, open inner parenthesis, a, plus c, close inner parenthesis, squared, close outer parenthesis, squared, end root, equals, open parenthesis, a, plus c, close parenthesis, squared, which equals a, squared, plus 2 a, c, plus c squared\"></span>.<!--[if--><!--[if--><!--[if--><!--[endif]--><!--[endif]--><!--[if--><!--[endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![if--><!--[if--><!--[endif]--><!--[endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![if--><!--![endif]--><!--![endif]--><!--![if--><!--![if--><p>\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--[if-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--[endif]-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![if-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![endif]-->\n        <!--![if-->\n        <!--![if-->\n        <!--![if-->Choice A is incorrect and may be the result of <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"the square root of, open parenthesis, a, plus c, close parenthesis, cubed, end root, divided by the square root of,  a, plus c, end root\"></span>. Choice B is incorrect and may be the result of incorrectly rewriting (<span class=\"italic\">a </span>+ <span class=\"italic\">c</span>)<sup>2</sup> as <span class=\"italic\">a</span><sup>2</sup> + <span class=\"italic\">c</span><sup>2</sup>. Choice D is incorrect and may be the result of incorrectly applying properties of exponents.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959635,"updateDate":1691007959635}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959635, 1691007959635),
    ($SATQ$e1391dd6$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$025772-DC$SATQ$, $SATQ$disclosed$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Nonlinear functions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p class="stem_paragraph ">According to Moore&rsquo;s law, the number of transistors included on microprocessors doubles every 2 years. In 1985, a microprocessor was introduced that had 275,000 transistors. Based on this information, in which of the following years does Moore&rsquo;s law estimate the number of transistors to reach 1.1&nbsp;million? </p>
$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1987</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1989</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1991</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1994</p>\n"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, NULL, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class="italic">x</span> be the number of years after 1985. It follows that <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_000_57b15dc1.png" alt="x over 2 "></span> represents the number of 2-year periods that will occur within an <span class="italic">x</span>-year period. According to Moore&rsquo;s law, every 2 years, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to double. Therefore, <span class="italic">x</span> years after 1985, the number of transistors will double <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_001_57b15dc1.png" alt="x over 2 "></span> times. Since the number of transistors included on a microprocessor was 275,000, or .275 million, in 1985, the estimated number of transistors, in millions, included <span class="italic">x</span> years after 1985 can be modeled as <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_002_c6fea035.png" alt="0 point 2 7 5 times 2 raised to the x over 2 power"></span>. The year in which the number of transistors is estimated to be 1.1 million is represented by the value of <span class="italic">x</span> when <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_003_e4641f96.png" alt="1 point 1 equals, 0 point 2 7 5, times 2 raised to the x over 2 power"></span>. Dividing both sides of this equation by .275 yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_004_63ede282.png" alt="4 equals, 2 raised to the x over 2 power"></span>, which can be rewritten as <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_005_21729bc5.png" alt="2 squared equals, 2 raised to the x over 2 power"></span>. Since the exponential equation has equal bases on each side, it follows that the exponents must also be equal: <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_006_682c515b.png" alt="2 equals x over 2"></span>. Multiplying both sides of the equation <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_007_682c515b.png" alt="2 equals x over 2"></span> by 2 yields <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_008_300bc77e.png" alt="x equals 4"></span>. Therefore, according to Moore&rsquo;s law, 4 years after 1985, or in 1989, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to reach 1.1 million.<p>Alternate approach: According to Moore&rsquo;s law, 2 years after 1985 (in 1987), the number of transistors included on a microprocessor is estimated to be <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_009_ab8203d9.png" alt="2 times 275,000"></span>, or 550,000, and 2 years after 1987 (in 1989), the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to be <span class="math-container"><img align="middle" role="math" class="math-img" src="https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_010_0d2c57eb.png" alt="2 times 550,000"></span>, or 1,100,000. Therefore, the year that Moore&rsquo;s law estimates the number of transistors on microprocessors to reach 1.1 million is 1989.</p><p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect. According to Moore&rsquo;s law, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to reach 550,000 in 1987, 2.2 million in 1991, and about 6.2 million in 1994.</p></p>
$SATQ$, true, 11, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$Multiple Choice$SATQ$, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ${"questionId":"e1391dd6","external_id":null,"disclosed_item_id":"025772-DC","source":"disclosed","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"P","domain":"Advanced Math","skill_cd":"P.C.","skill_desc":"Nonlinear functions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">According to Moore&rsquo;s law, the number of transistors included on microprocessors doubles every 2 years. In 1985, a microprocessor was introduced that had 275,000 transistors. Based on this information, in which of the following years does Moore&rsquo;s law estimate the number of transistors to reach 1.1&nbsp;million? </p>\n","answerOptions":[{"id":"a","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1987</p>\n"},{"id":"b","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1989</p>\n"},{"id":"c","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1991</p>\n"},{"id":"d","content":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1994</p>\n"}],"keys":null,"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">x</span> be the number of years after 1985. It follows that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_000_57b15dc1.png\" alt=\"x over 2 \"></span> represents the number of 2-year periods that will occur within an <span class=\"italic\">x</span>-year period. According to Moore&rsquo;s law, every 2 years, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to double. Therefore, <span class=\"italic\">x</span> years after 1985, the number of transistors will double <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_001_57b15dc1.png\" alt=\"x over 2 \"></span> times. Since the number of transistors included on a microprocessor was 275,000, or .275 million, in 1985, the estimated number of transistors, in millions, included <span class=\"italic\">x</span> years after 1985 can be modeled as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_002_c6fea035.png\" alt=\"0 point 2 7 5 times 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>. The year in which the number of transistors is estimated to be 1.1 million is represented by the value of <span class=\"italic\">x</span> when <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_003_e4641f96.png\" alt=\"1 point 1 equals, 0 point 2 7 5, times 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>. Dividing both sides of this equation by .275 yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_004_63ede282.png\" alt=\"4 equals, 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>, which can be rewritten as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_005_21729bc5.png\" alt=\"2 squared equals, 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>. Since the exponential equation has equal bases on each side, it follows that the exponents must also be equal: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_006_682c515b.png\" alt=\"2 equals x over 2\"></span>. Multiplying both sides of the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_007_682c515b.png\" alt=\"2 equals x over 2\"></span> by 2 yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_008_300bc77e.png\" alt=\"x equals 4\"></span>. Therefore, according to Moore&rsquo;s law, 4 years after 1985, or in 1989, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to reach 1.1 million.<p>Alternate approach: According to Moore&rsquo;s law, 2 years after 1985 (in 1987), the number of transistors included on a microprocessor is estimated to be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_009_ab8203d9.png\" alt=\"2 times 275,000\"></span>, or 550,000, and 2 years after 1987 (in 1989), the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"https://bkmyfcolrdumyrwktjrr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/sat-images/e1391dd6/img_010_0d2c57eb.png\" alt=\"2 times 550,000\"></span>, or 1,100,000. Therefore, the year that Moore&rsquo;s law estimates the number of transistors on microprocessors to reach 1.1 million is 1989.</p><p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect. According to Moore&rsquo;s law, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to reach 550,000 in 1987, 2.2 million in 1991, and about 6.2 million in 1994.</p></p>\n","answer_style":"Multiple Choice","section":"Math","image_count":11,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959619,"pPcc":"SAT#P","questionId":"e1391dd6","skill_cd":"P.C.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Nonlinear functions","createDate":1691007959619,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Advanced Math","ibn":"025772-DC","external_id":null,"primary_class_cd":"P","uId":"ac35345f-4ccc-4638-866d-3bef00c963c4","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"item_id":"025772-DC","section":"Math","prompt":"<p class=\"stem_paragraph \">According to Moore&rsquo;s law, the number of transistors included on microprocessors doubles every 2 years. In 1985, a microprocessor was introduced that had 275,000 transistors. Based on this information, in which of the following years does Moore&rsquo;s law estimate the number of transistors to reach 1.1&nbsp;million? </p>\n","answer":{"style":"Multiple Choice","choices":{"a":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1987</p>\n"},"b":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1989</p>\n"},"c":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1991</p>\n"},"d":{"body":"<p class=\"choice_paragraph \">1994</p>\n"}},"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. Let <span class=\"italic\">x</span> be the number of years after 1985. It follows that <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x over 2 \"></span> represents the number of 2-year periods that will occur within an <span class=\"italic\">x</span>-year period. According to Moore&rsquo;s law, every 2 years, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to double. Therefore, <span class=\"italic\">x</span> years after 1985, the number of transistors will double <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x over 2 \"></span> times. Since the number of transistors included on a microprocessor was 275,000, or .275 million, in 1985, the estimated number of transistors, in millions, included <span class=\"italic\">x</span> years after 1985 can be modeled as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"0 point 2 7 5 times 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>. The year in which the number of transistors is estimated to be 1.1 million is represented by the value of <span class=\"italic\">x</span> when <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"1 point 1 equals, 0 point 2 7 5, times 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>. Dividing both sides of this equation by .275 yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"4 equals, 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>, which can be rewritten as <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 squared equals, 2 raised to the x over 2 power\"></span>. Since the exponential equation has equal bases on each side, it follows that the exponents must also be equal: <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 equals x over 2\"></span>. Multiplying both sides of the equation <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 equals x over 2\"></span> by 2 yields <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"x equals 4\"></span>. Therefore, according to Moore&rsquo;s law, 4 years after 1985, or in 1989, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to reach 1.1 million.<p>Alternate approach: According to Moore&rsquo;s law, 2 years after 1985 (in 1987), the number of transistors included on a microprocessor is estimated to be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times 275,000\"></span>, or 550,000, and 2 years after 1987 (in 1989), the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to be <span class=\"math-container\"><img align=\"middle\" role=\"math\" class=\"math-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"2 times 550,000\"></span>, or 1,100,000. Therefore, the year that Moore&rsquo;s law estimates the number of transistors on microprocessors to reach 1.1 million is 1989.</p><p>Choices A, C, and D are incorrect. According to Moore&rsquo;s law, the number of transistors included on microprocessors is estimated to reach 550,000 in 1987, 2.2 million in 1991, and about 6.2 million in 1994.</p></p>\n"}},"createDate":1691007959619,"updateDate":1691007959619,"correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale":true}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959619, 1691007959619),
